**Read:** [Optical Mineralogy — Brock University course and laboratory material](../sources/Optical-Mineralogy-Brock.lingua#r_4HWN93T5EATAS40HNJGMCHSM7E), the complete biaxial indicatrix, figure, optic-sign, and associated laboratory material; [Mineralogy — Dexter Perkins, open editions](../sources/Mineralogy-Perkins.lingua#r_3Y3EJ93EG4V7Q0TZBCRRXVNVKP), the full biaxial optical treatment; [Guide to Thin Section Microscopy — Raith, Raase, and Reinhardt](../sources/Guide-to-Thin-Section-Microscopy.lingua#r_2MH30T4MGW8YMVP9SA3Z7XCDSD), the relevant complete observation/measurement procedures.

A biaxial indicatrix uses three principal refractive indices, conventionally ordered α ≤ β ≤ γ in the selected text. Two optic-axis directions and their angular relationship arise from the geometry; the measured apparent figure also depends on orientation, objective/aperture, wavelength, and specimen conditions.

Distinguish acute/obtuse bisectrix, optic-axis, and flash figures, including off-center cases. Isogyre movement under rotation is evidence requiring a geometrical interpretation. A small optic angle or an incomplete figure can make an apparently simple pattern ambiguous.

Optical sign identifies which principal direction corresponds to the acute bisectrix under the adopted convention. Dispersion, absorption, twinning, zoning, and strain can complicate observations. A numerical 2V estimate needs its actual measurement method and uncertainty rather than an unsupported value read from a schematic.

**Work:** complete the biaxial optics/figure labs. Construct indicatrix sections and explain a sign determination using the actual accessory and orientation procedure. Compare two candidate interpretations of an incomplete observed figure, identifying a discriminating rotation or complementary property. State where the uncertainty prevents an exact compositional inference.

Read all biaxial lectures and practical-guide sections before attempting unknowns. Combine the result with plane-light, crossed-light, texture, and chemical evidence rather than using optical sign as a complete mineral name.

